  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNCAC implementation globally UNODC</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support through United Nations office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) for the implementation of commitments in the UN Convention against corruption (UNCAC)  to the Global Anti-corruption Programme for accelerating implementation of UNCAC. This also include support to partners such as Stolen Asset Recovery Initiative (StAR) which is a collaboration between World Bank and UNODC, Open Ownership for activities related to Beneficial Ownership Transparency (registries), and Open Contracting Partnership for activities related to transparent and effective public procurement. 

Through the support technical assistance is provided, and knowledge products developed and used.  UNODC will also provide training and advisory services based upon request from countries. Regional hubs will be used for a flexible approach.  

The fight against corruption is ultimately about fairness and trust because the impact of corruption on society is a distortion in real policy outcomes against the intended policy outcomes the public trusts governments to deliver. Among the most pressing challenges of our time are equity and sustainability. Equity - including gender equality - is entirely about fairness and the trust that every member of society has a fair chance to improve their social and economic opportunities and wellbeing. When public procurement markets are distorted through corruption, disadvantaged groups are those most directly impacted. This affects citizens from two main perspectives -  suppliers bidding on tenders and citizens receiving public services. Sustainability and the fight against climate change requires all of us to completely change how we consume and buy things -- including governments who are the world&#x2019;s biggest spenders. From reducing plastics  to new markets for green energy to prioritizing suppliers with lower carbon footprints to building green infrastructure to natural disaster preparedness the challenge of making public procurement as sustainable as possible is vast.</narrative>
  </description>
